<h1>Hardware Acceleration Practices in HFT</h1>
<p class="lead">Why this matters: Lesson 0002 covered FPGAs and 0011 placed them on the latency ladder. This lesson zooms out to the full menu of hardware accelerators a trading firm actually uses — FPGA, GPU, SmartNIC/DPU, ASIC, NPU — and which workload goes where.</p>
<p class="illus">Illustrative code where the repo doesn't have the real thing yet.</p>

<h2>1. The five-family taxonomy</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Family</th><th>What it is</th><th>Best at</th><th>HFT role</th></tr>
<tr><td>CPU</td><td>General-purpose serial cores with deep caches and branch prediction.</td><td>Complex branchy logic, glue code.</td><td>Strategy logic on tier 2; control plane everywhere.</td></tr>
<tr><td>FPGA</td><td>Reconfigurable logic fabric.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-1">[1]</a></sup></td><td>Deterministic ns-scale pipelines.</td><td>Tick-to-trade hot path (this repo's <code>fpga/</code>).</td></tr>
<tr><td>GPU</td><td>Thousands of SIMD cores, deep memory hierarchy.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-2">[2]</a></sup></td><td>Massively parallel dense math (training, batch inference).</td><td>Model training, alt-data CV, options pricing. Not hot path.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SmartNIC / DPU</td><td>NIC with onboard ARM cores + programmable packet engines (BlueField, Pensando).<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-3">[3]</a></sup></td><td>Network offload, in-line risk checks, parsing.</td><td>Replacing dumb NICs in colocation racks; growing fast.</td></tr>
<tr><td>ASIC / NPU</td><td>Fixed-function silicon for one job. NPUs are AI-specific ASICs.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-4">[4]</a></sup></td><td>Best $/throughput/watt for one workload.</td><td>Apex HFT for matching/parsing; AI training at hyperscalers.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>2. The decision framework — three questions</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="card"><h4>1. Determinism vs throughput?</h4><p>If the workload is "one tick at a time, ns matter" → FPGA/ASIC. If it's "millions of samples, batched" → GPU/NPU. CPUs sit between, mediocre at both.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>2. How often does the algorithm change?</h4><p>Weekly strategy iteration → FPGA (re-synth in hours). Frozen algorithm at scale → ASIC (months of tape-out, then years of use).</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>3. Is the workload AI?</h4><p>Training: GPU is still king (NVIDIA H100/B100). Inference at low latency: NPU/Groq LPU offer 10×+ vs GPU on specific shapes.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-2">[2]</a></sup> Inference on the trading hot path: hls4ml-style FPGA compile.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>The pattern: heterogeneous</h4><p>2025 production HFT stacks combine all five — CPU control + FPGA hot path + GPU training + SmartNIC for risk/parsing + (rarely) ASIC for matching. Mixing tiers is the design, not a smell.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-1">[1]</a></sup></p></div>
</div>

<h2>3. SmartNIC / DPU — the newest entrant worth understanding</h2>
<p>A SmartNIC is a NIC plus an embedded CPU complex (typically ARM) and programmable packet engines. NVIDIA BlueField and AMD Pensando are the market leaders; BlueField-4 ships 800 Gbps with 6× compute over its predecessor.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-3">[3]</a></sup> The DPU/SmartNIC market reached $1.11B in 2024 and is on a 14.9% CAGR through 2034.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-3">[3]</a></sup></p>
<p>For HFT specifically they fit two niches:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Inline risk checks.</strong> Run Rule 15c3-5 pre-trade filters on the DPU's ARM cores instead of on the host CPU, saving the PCIe round-trip for rejected orders.</li>
<li><strong>Protocol parsing.</strong> Strip exchange feeds (ITCH, OUCH, FIX) on the DPU and hand the application a structured tick — same shape as FPGA parsing but cheaper to ship a new schema (no re-synth).</li>
</ul>

<h2>4. The repo today and the realistic next step</h2>
<p>The repo's hardware story is the FPGA folder (<code>fpga/*.vhd</code>). Realistic upgrade paths, in order of cost:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Add a GPU for training</strong> — the GBM in <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> stays on CPU; if you ever swap it for a neural net (Lesson 0007), training moves to GPU. Zero hot-path impact.</li>
<li><strong>Add a SmartNIC for the eventual feed handler</strong> — when (if) the repo gains a live exchange feed, BlueField-class NICs let you offload parsing without writing HDL.</li>
<li><strong>Compile a small NN onto the FPGA via hls4ml</strong> — covered in Lesson 0007. The bridge between Python-trained models and ULL execution.</li>
<li><strong>ASIC: don't.</strong> Not at this scale; the economics need billions of messages per day to justify tape-out.</li>
</ol>

<h2>5. Often confused with</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="card"><h4>DPU vs SmartNIC</h4><p>The terms are used interchangeably in 2025 marketing. Pedantically: SmartNIC = NIC with some programmability; DPU = SmartNIC with a full CPU complex and OS. NVIDIA/AMD now use "DPU" exclusively.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-3">[3]</a></sup></p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>FPGA vs ASIC</h4><p>FPGA reprograms in seconds via a new bitstream; ASIC is silicon you taped out and frozen. Same logic on ASIC is 5–10× faster and ~10× cheaper per unit at scale but $10M+ to make.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>NPU vs GPU</h4><p>NPU is an ASIC specialized for the matmul + activation pattern of neural nets. Better $/W on that shape; useless for general parallel code. GPU is more flexible, more power-hungry.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-4">[4]</a></sup></p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Acceleration vs Offload</h4><p><strong>Acceleration</strong>: doing the same work faster. <strong>Offload</strong>: moving the work elsewhere so the original processor can do other things. SmartNICs are mostly offload; FPGAs are mostly acceleration.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>6. Practice</h2>
<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q1. You need to run a 7B-parameter LLM for nightly news-sentiment featurization across millions of articles. Which accelerator?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="FPGAs are deterministic and ns-scale but terrible for huge batched dense matmuls — wrong workload shape.">FPGA via hls4ml.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right — overnight batch inference of dense models is exactly the GPU/NPU sweet spot. Cold path, throughput-bound, no determinism need.">GPU (or NPU if cost matters).</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="CPU works but takes ~50–100× longer. For nightly batch you'd want a GPU on AWS p4d or similar.">CPU with AVX-512.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="ASICs are for fixed-function high-volume work like a single matching engine — not for varying inference workloads.">Custom ASIC.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q2. The Rule-15c3-5 pre-trade risk check is currently on the host CPU and adds ~3 µs per order. You want to halve it without changing the rule logic. Best option?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Putting the same risk filter on a SmartNIC's ARM cores avoids the host PCIe round-trip for rejected orders and runs the logic at line rate. No new logic, just a different location.">Move the filter to a SmartNIC / DPU so it runs inline at line rate.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Rule 15c3-5 explicitly prohibits skipping the filter. Removing it is illegal regardless of latency benefit.">Skip the risk check on small orders.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="GPUs are throughput devices with terrible single-message latency due to driver and PCIe round-trip — usually worse than CPU for one event.">Run the check on a GPU.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Hand-translating a checkable filter rule into VHDL works but takes weeks and is harder to audit than the same C code on a DPU.">Rewrite the entire rule in VHDL on the FPGA.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q3. Why isn't ASIC the answer for almost any small-to-medium HFT desk?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="ASICs are reliable once made — that's not the issue. Tape-out economics are.">ASICs have higher fault rates than FPGAs.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right — a single tape-out at modern node costs $5–50M, takes 12–24 months, and freezes the design forever. Only firms with a stable, multi-year-justifiable workload (e.g. a matching engine, an exchange) make ASICs work. HFT strategies iterate too fast.">Tape-out costs millions and takes a year-plus, and the design is frozen — strategies iterate too fast to amortize that.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="ASICs are widely available in design-services arrangements; the issue is whether a desk's strategy economics support the capex.">ASICs are export-controlled and unavailable to most firms.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="ASICs are faster, not slower. The blocker is cost and inflexibility, not performance.">ASICs are slower than FPGAs for the same workload.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q4. A modern production HFT stack typically uses how many of these accelerator families simultaneously?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="A single-accelerator stack is naive. Each family fits a different niche and pure-FPGA shops still need CPUs for control and GPUs for offline ML.">One — usually FPGA, with everything else replaced.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Heterogeneous architectures combining CPU + FPGA + GPU + SmartNIC (and occasionally NPU/ASIC) are the 2025 norm because each family fits a different niche on the same firm's workload graph.">Three to five, with each family serving a different niche on the firm's workload graph.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Two might describe a small startup but production HFT firms run heterogeneous stacks with GPUs for research, CPUs for control, FPGAs for hot path, SmartNICs for offload, and increasingly NPUs for AI inference.">Exactly two — CPU and FPGA, since the others are research-only.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="ASICs are at the apex and rare; the broader stack uses the other four families heavily. 'All ASIC' is not how anyone operates.">All ASIC — anything programmable is a transitional technology.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<h2>7. Scenarios</h2>
<details class="scenario"><summary>S1. A founder claims their new "AI HFT platform" will use only NPUs for everything. What's wrong with the architecture story?</summary>
<p>NPUs are inference-shaped — fixed matmul + activation pipelines on dense tensors. They don't run general code well, they don't do deterministic ns-scale parsing well, they don't replace control-plane CPUs, and they don't compete with FPGAs on tick-to-trade. "All NPU" can't be the architecture; it can only be the inference accelerator inside a heterogeneous stack. Treat the claim as marketing.</p></details>

<details class="scenario"><summary>S2. Your team wants to add a SmartNIC. Which workload should NOT be on the DPU?</summary>
<p>The actual strategy decision (the buy/sell/hold logic) and any complex branching state machine. SmartNICs excel at line-rate stateless or shallow-state transforms — parsing, filtering, simple risk checks, encryption offload. Putting the full strategy on a DPU's ARM core gives you a slow CPU, not an accelerator. Strategy stays on the FPGA or a tier-2 CPU; offload the boring inline plumbing to the DPU.</p></details>

